~ tongue: bare
~ kernel/speaker.glo — `burn` and `silence`: sound from the PC speaker.
~
~ The original PC had one loudspeaker and no sound card. It is wired to a
~ single wire that the CPU can flip on and off, and that wire can instead be
~ fed from channel 2 of the same timer chip that gives us milliseconds
~ (kernel/timer.glo). Channel 2 in "square wave" mode flips its output every
~ N input ticks, so the speaker's pitch is 1,193,182 / N Hz.
~
~ Three steps to make a note:
~   1. tell the PIT's command port (0x43) "channel 2, 16-bit count, square wave";
~   2. write the count N, low byte then high byte, to channel 2's port (0x42);
~   3. open the gate: port 0x61 bit 0 lets the timer run, bit 1 connects it to
~      the speaker. Silence is clearing those two bits.
~
~ QEMU emulates the speaker (qemu-system-x86 -machine pcspk-audiodev=...),
~ so this really does make a sound, with no sound-card driver at all.
invoke "timer"

rite
  incant speaker_off
    let gate be listen port 0x61
    whisper p8 (call band with gate, 0xFC) to port 0x61
  end

  incant speaker_on takes hz
    ~ outside the range a speaker can usefully play, make no sound (and
    ~ never divide by zero)
    if hz below 20 or hz above 20000
      call speaker_off
      void
    end
    let divisor be call udiv with 1193182, hz
    whisper p8 0xB6 to port 0x43
    whisper p8 (call band with divisor, 255) to port 0x42
    whisper p8 (call shr with divisor, 8) to port 0x42
    let gate be listen port 0x61
    whisper p8 (call bor with gate, 3) to port 0x61
  end

  ~ the hooks the `burn` / `silence` statements compile to
  incant t_burn takes hz
    call speaker_on with hz
  end

  incant t_silence
    call speaker_off
  end
end
